#%PAM-1.0
# Script function and purpose: PAM policy for Sakura's normal login on
# FreeBSD. It defers wholly to the system login stack.

# Deferring to `include login` is the whole policy, and it is deliberate: the
# base system's own stack is what a console login already uses, so Sakura
# authenticates by exactly the rules the rest of the machine does, and anything
# the administrator configures there applies here without being restated.
#
# `session include login` is also what provides XDG_RUNTIME_DIR: that stack
# reaches /etc/pam.d/system, which names pam_xdg(8), and pam_xdg creates
# /var/run/xdg/$USER and publishes the variable into the PAM environment.
# Sakura re-exports it, and a Wayland compositor cannot create its socket
# without it, so this is load-bearing for a graphical session and not merely
# conventional.
#
# Nothing here needs the leading "-" (non-fatal module) prefix, which OpenPAM
# only understands from FreeBSD 15.0 -- earlier releases fail the entire policy
# on an unrecognised facility rather than skipping the line. Since 15.0 is the
# baseline the prefix is available if a future addition wants it. The Linux
# policy stays a separate file because it names distribution-specific modules
# that have no FreeBSD counterpart, not because of that prefix.
auth       include      login
account    include      login
password   include      login
session    include      login
